  "context": "Edited and published by Friedrich Engels in 1894, Volume 3 presents the concrete forms that capital assumes in society as a whole: the conversion of surplus value into profit, the transformation of values into prices of production, commercial profit, interest, credit, and ground rent.",
  "relatesToToday": "Explains financial market bubbles, skyrocketing housing costs, and bank bailouts. When real estate investors buy up residential homes to charge high rents, or when tech startups rely on venture capital debt rather than real production, capital seeks speculative profits because factory manufacturing rates of profit tend to decline.",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Competition equalizes rates of profit across industries, transforming commodity values into prices of production.",
    "Rising organic composition of capital causes a tendential fall in the general rate of profit, triggering counteracting forces.",
    "Credit and fictitious capital expand the scope of capitalist production beyond immediate cash reserves but amplify economic instability."
